{
  "file": "crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs",
  "item": "impl[StoredGraph/Graph]/fn[outgoing_edges]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "Contents requires this callback even for a storage-only consumer. The\n delegation test must use only node_path; discovering edges would be\n an unwanted graph access, which this guard rejects. Traversal fixtures\n separately exercise edge discovery."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Graph]/fn[outgoing_edges]` is a valid coverage exception.\n\n- **Form:** It is a fenced citation commentary on an explicit function with a body. That is a permitted form, and the commentary counts as a tracked justification.\n- **Substance:** The reason is that the fixture needs `outgoing_edges` only to satisfy the trait. The storage delegation test must use only `node_path`, so the function is a guard that panics if anything discovers edges. Difficulty is not the reason given.\n- **Trait claim:** The trait requirement is real. `Contents` is declared as `pub trait Contents: Graph` in `crates/habeas-sdk/src/graph.rs#trait[Contents]`. `StoredGraph` implements `Contents`, so it must also provide `Graph::outgoing_edges`.\n- **Delegation test:** `consumer_delegates_evidence_storage_using_only_the_requested_nodes_path` calls only `load_evidence` and `store_evidence`. Those delegate through `node_path`. Neither calls `outgoing_edges`, so the guard is never run, which matches the commentary.\n- **Traversal fixtures:** Other fixtures do exercise edge discovery with real implementations. Examples are `impl[Memory/Graph]/fn[outgoing_edges]` in `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/agent.rs` and in `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/validation.rs`.\n- **Redundancy:** The function has no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference to the cited built-in, so the citation is not redundant.",
